# <%= @app_title %>

<!-- One or two sentences on what this app does and who uses it. -->

## Commands
- Setup: `bin/setup`
- Run: `bin/dev` (port 3000)
- Tests: `bin/rails test` (one file: `bin/rails test test/models/user_test.rb`; system tests: `bin/rails test:system`)
- Lint: `bin/rubocop -a` (runs automatically after every Ruby edit via a hook)
- Security: `bin/brakeman --no-pager`

## Stack
- Rails 8, PostgreSQL, Hotwire (Turbo + Stimulus), importmap, Solid Queue/Cache/Cable
- Minitest + fixtures (not RSpec, not FactoryBot)
- rubocop-rails-omakase style

## Conventions
- Follow the `vanilla-rails` skill: CRUD resources only, thin controllers, rich models, one concern per behaviour
- New state changes are new resources (`resource :archival`), never custom actions (`post :archive`)
- No service objects by default; a plain Ruby object in app/models is fine when justified
- Every model change comes with a migration and tests

## Rules
- Write the failing test first, then the code
- Run the relevant tests and `bin/rubocop` before saying a task is done
- Never edit `db/schema.rb` by hand; never run destructive DB commands
- Use Tidewave's tools to evaluate code and query the database; use `get_docs` for documentation and `get_source_location` to find definitions
<% if @multi_tenant -%>

## Multi-tenancy
- Every tenant-owned query goes through `Current.account` (e.g. `Current.account.projects.find(id)`), never `Project.find` or `Project.all`
- See the `multi-tenancy` skill before adding a model, controller or job
<% end -%>
<% if @use_fizzy -%>

## Reference implementation
Fizzy (37signals) is checked out read-only at <%= @fizzy_path %>.
When unsure how to structure a model, concern, controller, job or test, find the
closest equivalent there and follow its shape. This file wins where they differ
(we use PostgreSQL; Fizzy uses SQLite/MySQL and UUID keys). Look at specific
folders; don't read the whole repo.
<% end -%>
